 "raw_reply": "{\"item\": \"S443-025\", \"clauses\": {\"determine\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F10\", \"template\": \"Determine the total value of {desc}:\", \"reasoning\": \"F10 asks the learner to determine the total value of a pile description built from F19 or F20, which print counted coin and bill piles. The task wording matches the clause.\"}, \"write_value\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F16\", \"template\": \"What is the total value of {desc}?\", \"reasoning\": \"F16 asks for the total value and expects a written number. Its desc is drawn from F17 or F19, so a learner writes the value of the piles.\"}, \"number_of_bills\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F16\", \"template\": \"What is the total value of {desc}?\", \"reasoning\": \"F17 can print the unit word bills with a count, and F16 presents that pile as the problem. This produces problems about a number of bills. The unit is chosen independently of the face, so the wording of these problems is defective (see failing_families), but the clause is producible.\"}, \"number_of_coins\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F16\", \"template\": \"What is the total value of {desc}?\", \"reasoning\": \"F17 can print the unit word coins with a count, and F16 presents that pile as the problem. This produces problems about a number of coins. The unit-face defect is recorded under failing_families.\"}, \"combination_of_bills_and_coins\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F14\", \"template\": \"How much is {desc} altogether?\", \"reasoning\": \"F14 with desc from F20 joins two F17 piles with the template for two values, so one problem can combine a coin pile and a bill pile.\"}, \"up_to_1000\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F08\", \"template\": \"Use coins and bills to make exactly ₱{total}.\", \"reasoning\": \"The total slot repeats the generated total, with printed runs reaching 1000, so the range up to ₱1000 is produced. The peso sign on centavo totals is a defect recorded under failing_families.\"}, \"centavo_coins_only\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F15\", \"template\": \"What is the total value of {desc}, in centavos?\", \"reasoning\": \"F17 prints the centavo face through F18 with denominations 25 and 50, and the centavo unit in F15 matches centavo-only problems. The face-unit mismatch and the in-centavos conversion of other piles are recorded under failing_families.\"}, \"peso_coins_only\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F16\", \"template\": \"What is the total value of {desc}?\", \"reasoning\": \"F17 prints peso coin faces through F21 with denominations 1, 5, 10, 20 and the coin unit, and F16 presents the pile as the problem.\"}, \"peso_bills_only\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F16\", \"template\": \"What is the total value of {desc}?\", \"reasoning\": \"F21 prints the peso faces 20, 50, 100, 200, 500, and F17 can pair them with the bill unit, so F16 produces peso bill problems. The unit-face defect is recorded under failing_families.\"}, \"combined_peso_coins_and_peso_bills\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F14\", \"template\": \"How much is {desc} altogether?\", \"reasoning\": \"F14 with desc from F19 or F20 joins piles drawn from F17, which can use peso coin and peso bill faces in one problem.\"}}, \"failing_families\": [\"F02\", \"F03\", \"F06\", \"F08\", \"F09\", \"F10\", \"F11\", \"F12\", \"F13\", \"F14\", \"F15\", \"F16\", \"F17\", \"F19\", \"F20\"], \"failing_templates\": [\"Step {v}: Add {peso_label}{amt} → running total = {peso_label}{running}.\", \"The total is {peso_label}{total}.\", \"The dot is at ₱{a} and it moves {direction} ₱{b}. Which amount will the dot land on?\", \"Use coins and bills to make exactly ₱{total}.\", \"{stem} Use the fewest pieces possible.\", \"Determine the total value of {desc}:\", \"Find the total amount of {desc}, in centavos:\", \"Find the total amount of {desc}:\", \"How much is {desc} altogether, in centavos?\", \"How much is {desc} altogether?\", \"What is the total value of {desc}, in centavos?\", \"What is the total value of {desc}?\", \"{count} {face} {unit}\", \"{join}, and {v}\", \"{v} and {v2}\"], \"overall\": \"FAIL\", \"reasoning\": \"Family F17 prints a count, a face and a unit, and the unit is a generator choice from bill, bills, coin and coins with no link to the face or the count. A problem can therefore print a centavo face or a peso coin face with the word bill, or a count of 1 with the plural unit. The wording is then wrong for the learner. Families F10 through F16, F19 and F20 print F17 text inside their own templates, so they carry the same fault. Families F02 and F03 print the peso label with each amount, and the amounts domain includes centavo values 1, 5, 10, 25 and 50, so a centavo amount reads as pesos. F06 and F08 print the peso sign before the printed runs, which include 25 and 50, with the same effect. F06 also asks the learner to move a dot forward or back by an amount, an operation the competency does not name. F09 requires the fewest pieces, and F11, F13 and F15 require expressing peso amounts in centavos; the competency names neither. F04 prints a text computed from the amounts whose form the packet does not show, so it is not failed here. F01, F05, F07, F18 and F21 are not failing on their own. Declared bounds and vocabulary gates are satisfied on the packet's face.\"}",
